{
  "interpretation": "The plan refactors app/db.py to replace per-request SQLite connection overhead with a thread-local connection pool. Validation confirms the patch is correct and functional. Measurements show the candidate implementation achieved a 9.69% relative improvement in latency (mean 0.512ms vs parent 0.562ms) across 6000 operations with zero failures or semantic mismatches. The runner accepted the change based on this improvement exceeding the epsilon threshold. The pool implementation uses double-checked locking for initialization and validates connection health via SELECT 1. While the mean latency decreased, the candidate's p95 latency (0.670ms) and max latency (4.38ms) are higher than the parent's (0.720ms p95, 4.11ms max), suggesting increased tail latency variance despite better average performance. The noise coefficient also increased from 0.01 to 0.025, indicating less consistent performance under load.",
  "limitations": [
    "Tail latency (p95/max) increased, indicating potential variance issues with the pooled connections.",
    "The noise coefficient doubled, suggesting less stable performance consistency.",
    "SQLite locking risks remain unmitigated for concurrent write scenarios.",
    "Epsilon threshold was not explicitly defined, relying on relative improvement metric."
  ],
  "schema_version": 1
}
